From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 23:25:20 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 40/60] hjviewer: minimum version: the update screens
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The screens of the signed version policy (design minver 2-1, 2-3, 2-5, 3-1,
5-1 to 5-5, 9-3), written against the Rust core's main_get_common and
main_set_common keys, so the generated bridge does not change. A build
without the update keys answers "" for hjv-update-state, and every hook then
leaves its screen as 1.5.1 has it.

- The gate: an app below its platform's minimum gets an update screen in
  place of the connection page's outgoing side; connect(), newSession and
  newTerminal refuse to start a session and say so (B1). The verdict is the
  core's, read at once in every window; when the policy was last asked for
  over half an hour ago, a connection waits up to two seconds for a new
  answer. Incoming connections, the settings, the connection manager, the
  install page and running sessions are left alone.
- A line above the connection page for a newer version and for the
  deadline in the device's own time (N1, N2), and the note about moving to
  Google Play (M1).
- The update screen per platform and install source: an installed Windows
  PC asks its service to install now after a confirmation (W1, W2, W4-W6);
  a Windows app that is not installed fetches the new file into Downloads
  and hands over to it (W3); an Android app from Google Play opens the Play
  Store; one from a file downloads into the app's cache and, while the app
  is in front (Android 10 and later open no installer for an app in the
  background), hands the checked APK to MainActivity's hjv_install_apk
  (WP-C5), or below Android 7.0 opens a short link in the browser; an
  iPhone opens TestFlight when the build was exported for it
  (HJV_IOS_EXPORT), else says whom to ask (I1-I3). A system too old for
  the new version is told so (O1).
- Checks: at the start after telling the core the device (install source,
  system version), when the app comes back to the front after an hour,
  right after a login, and by hand.
- A controller that connects to a Windows PC below the minimum hears how
  that PC is updated (C1a, C1b) on a card over the session: an installed
  PC by [지금 업데이트], one that runs without being installed by [업데이트]
  and [지금 실행]; for a PC without an update screen it can make a short
  link for the PC's browser, shown large and copied (U1, U2). Android peers
  get no notice: an updated Android app is no longer controlled.
- Dialogs make no links of addresses in the texts of servers and peers
  (D9), and Settings -> About shows the versions, the policy and the last
  check, with [업데이트 확인].
- A failure is told by the core's code; a downloaded file whose own version
  is not the policy's ("version") is told as one that is not this app's new
  version (E4).

Known limits: while a connection waits for the policy (two seconds at
most, when the last check is over half an hour old), another start for the
same PC, a file transfer beside a remote screen say, is dropped without a
word; it starts when tried again. The wait is held per PC so that a second
click does not open the same session twice.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
